1
00:00:00,700 --> 00:00:04,067
What happens when writing code becomes faster than moving it?

2
00:00:04,400 --> 00:00:08,033
Anthropic says its daily merges grew from about twenty-five

3
00:00:08,033 --> 00:00:11,300
hundred in May to over thirty thousand in September.

4
00:00:11,633 --> 00:00:15,000
More code means more work for everything downstream.

5
00:00:17,467 --> 00:00:21,700
Git gives you a local history you can inspect without asking a server.

6
00:00:22,067 --> 00:00:26,500
You can compare versions, create commits, and keep working offline.

7
00:00:26,867 --> 00:00:29,867
Think of a workshop with its own reference library: the

8
00:00:29,867 --> 00:00:33,233
information stays close to the person doing the work.

9
00:00:35,633 --> 00:00:38,000
A clone does not always have to bring everything.

10
00:00:38,333 --> 00:00:40,267
Shallow clones limit history.

11
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,633
Partial clones can leave file contents on the server until needed.

12
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:47,533
Sparse checkouts limit the working tree.

13
00:00:47,900 --> 00:00:53,467
These solve different problems, and choosing one does not automatically give you the others.

14
00:00:55,900 --> 00:01:00,333
Scion shifts the contract: clients stay online, keep a bounded

15
00:01:00,333 --> 00:01:04,800
cache, and delegate history, merges, and commits to the server.

16
00:01:05,167 --> 00:01:07,933
The design question changes from what each machine

17
00:01:08,067 --> 00:01:10,600
can do alone to what it actually needs nearby.

18
00:01:13,067 --> 00:01:15,500
Picture a test that only needs a parser.

19
00:01:15,900 --> 00:01:19,000
First, it checks for the requested object locally.

20
00:01:19,367 --> 00:01:22,100
If the object is missing, it asks upstream.

21
00:01:22,467 --> 00:01:24,533
The file appears where the test needs it.

22
00:01:24,867 --> 00:01:27,967
A later request can reuse that cached object.

23
00:01:30,433 --> 00:01:34,800
Scion also exposes source-control queries as APIs.

24
00:01:35,133 --> 00:01:38,767
A job can ask for a diff without creating a working copy.

25
00:01:39,100 --> 00:01:44,667
It creates changesets on the server: changed paths point to content-addressed file data.

26
00:01:47,133 --> 00:01:51,400
Files move through node, regional, and server caches.

27
00:01:51,767 --> 00:01:57,967
Immutable objects make reuse straightforward: the same identifier refers to the same content.

28
00:01:58,333 --> 00:02:00,967
Imagine many tests reading the same parser.

29
00:02:01,333 --> 00:02:06,267
Reusing the nearby copy avoids sending the same bytes on every trip.

30
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:13,500
Anthropic reports a two-hundred-fold smaller CPU and memory footprint for

31
00:02:13,500 --> 00:02:18,900
source-control infrastructure, and a twenty-five percent CPU capacity gain in CI.

32
00:02:19,233 --> 00:02:20,933
These are results from its own workload.

33
00:02:23,333 --> 00:02:28,633
Scion is specialized for enormous monorepos, not a general Git replacement.

34
00:02:28,967 --> 00:02:32,233
Its surrounding development tools also had to change.

35
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,667
For your own system, start with the job: what must remain local,

36
00:02:37,033 --> 00:02:41,067
what can be shared, and what happens when the network is unavailable?

37
00:02:43,500 --> 00:02:48,700
A faster code generator does not automatically create a faster development system.

38
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:54,067
Trace the whole path: write, retrieve, build, test, review.

39
00:02:54,400 --> 00:02:56,900
Measure where time and resources go.

40
00:02:57,233 --> 00:03:03,167
Then move less data, reuse more of it, and give each operation only the work it needs.
